<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="preface">Preface</h2>

  <p><strong>Foreword:</strong> Words coming before a preface.<br>
  <strong>Forward:</strong> The way your nose points or eager.</p>

  <p>Telling that to a computer is like telling your wife someone else was wearing your shirt when it got lipstick on the collar. I don't own a computer nor do I wish to own one. My sister Pauline Jackson Bates and her daughters operate computers and advise me to avail myself of these metal and glass geniuses in my writings. In the final phase of my last publication I engaged a local firm to retype the stories which were placed by some form of magic on a computer disk to be used by the publisher. It took five weeks of pleading to convince the computer that foreword and forward have different meanings.</p>

  <p>A foreword or preface is an introductory to the reader with enticement enough to get the reader to read beyond the preface. I could begin with "Mayor Sprains Knee in Back Seat Romp With Church Organist". Now that would get your attention and you would flip a few pages to see if there were pictures or if a shooting followed but that would be misleading. Actually it was the organist who suffered the sprained knee, the pictures were underexposed and her husband was in a motel room with a barmaid at the time.</p>

  <p>The previous is untrue. If you read beyond this you will find stories that were humorous enough for me to remember them. The characters are real people and the events actually happened though you need to be aware the author is old, senile and known to exaggerate as well as outright lie if it better serves his purpose. Every person has a story to tell if we just take the time to listen. I was born and grew up in Creede, Colorado, in an era of poverty when a sense of humor was the greatest asset a person could have to survive each day. The oldsters were patient with children if they were respectful and quiet. They were our main source of knowledge and humor, their stories were priceless and a positive influence on those of us who listened.</p>

  <p>Please read on. Your name or a name of someone you know may be mentioned. We all deserve to be remembered because we have all served a purpose in this life. Success is measured in many and varied ways, not the least of which is to be offensive and loud enough to be remembered.</p>
</section>

<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="dedication">Dedication</h2>

  <p>Real books always have a page reserved where the author gives thanks to those who contributed in some way during his climb to mediocrity and insolvency. We shall conform though this publication is not to be construed as a real book.</p>

  <p>To my loyal wife, Inez Schriver Jackson, who was advised to kill me 50 years ago and tried but could never find a sharp knife.</p>

  <p>To my loyal sister, Pauline "Bubbles" Bates, who prevented the killings by dulling the knives and alerting me to danger.</p>

  <p>To Dr. Tarr of Gunnison, Dr. Adkisson of Delta and Dr. Lambert of Grand Junction who expertly repaired the dull knife wounds.</p>

  <p>To Dr. Huene of Delta, who fixed my running gear.</p>

  <p>To dear friends of the U.S. Geological Survey who have shared their knowledge, their children and many laughs.</p>

  <p>To my compatriots in Freeport Exploration Company who tried to kill me in such a manner that Inez would receive double indemnity from my insurance policy.</p>

  <p>To the dear souls of Creede, Colorado and those I met in my journeys who misbehaved with such gusto that I remembered their foul deeds.</p>

  <p>To the dear souls of Creede, Colorado who did not misbehave and inspired me to rise above the gutter. I tried but the others wouldn't let go of my ankles.</p>

  <p>I believe you all know which category you belong in. I haven't named you, personally, to spare you embarrassment and I would advise you that a little extra tithing may prevent the names coming up in any future publication. Cash is preferred.</p>

  <p>Finally, to Dad Bill and Mom Jo, who never gave up hope that I would make them proud and graciously passed on with that vain hope. I have no excuse. They gave me the proper equipment but the instructions were left in the Stork's basket and went to another child.</p>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>Front Cover: In fond memory of my pards, Floyd Dooley and Eugene Wardell, top left and center (author on right), best friends, top miners, who left us too soon. Lower, left to right, 'rangutang, she ghost and stope ape.</em></p>
</section>

<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="hell-cats-and-she-ghosts-origin">Hell Cats And She Ghosts: Origin</h2>

  <p>A hell cat and a she ghost: Anything incomprehensible that defies the imagination. Anything that scared the hell out of you or a person behaving in a reprehensible manner offensive to his fellow man.</p>

  <p>The descriptive phrase was coined by Lynn "Key" LeZotte, a native son of Creede Colorado who shared "Hell Cat and She Ghost" stories with me when we worked together shoveling concentrates at a lead/silver mill and, later, blasting ore to go to the same reduction mill from mines near Creede.</p>

  <p>Key was honest, truthful, hard working and always a gentleman in mixed company, the most splendid attributes a person can have. He was burdened with a variety of handicaps as a child from a bout with scarlet fever but he overcame all, was steadily employed and a friend to all who knew him.</p>

  <p>We were at an Elk's dance one night, both of us "three sheets in the wind", when he let out a ferocious war whoop and rapped me between the eyes with the butt end of a half-full bottle of beer. Evidently he thought I was behaving like a hell cat and a she ghost and decided to straighten me out which he effectively did, flat on my back. Key felt badly about that. I felt worse with a goose egg above my nose and two blackened eyes. After sobering up and going over the events that led to our momentary disagreement we felt there was no credible way to explain our behavior to these not associated with hell cats and she ghosts and we should just forget about it. And we did that until this writing and long after Key's death.</p>

  <p>Lynn "Key" LeZotte, wherever you are, know that we miss you and if we are worthy of being together with you again, put in a good word for us. There are still hell cats and she ghosts around and some of them deserve to be straightened out with the butt end of a beer bottle but we must be politically correct in this era and cannot set the stage with a "war whoop"</p>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>Printed page 1 (unnumbered in print). "These" for "those," and the missing closing quotation mark after "war whoop," are printed as shown.</em></p>
</section>
